\par {\*\bkmkstart pgfId_374316}{\*\bkmkend pgfId_374316}The file generated.txt includes a list of all the files generated by the wizard. 
\par {\*\bkmkstart pgfId_371121}{\*\bkmkend pgfId_371121}The file }{\i\f1 component}{\f1 .}{\i\f1 log}{\f1  contains a summary of the processing done by the w
izard. The name of the file is the name of the definition file appended with log (so Figure 10 presumes that the definition file was e.g. }{\i\f1 component}{\f1 .}{\i\f1 idl}{\f1 ) 
\par The folder }{\i\f1 idl}{\f1  contains the generated IDL file.
\par {\*\bkmkstart pgfId_371124}{\*\bkmkend pgfId_371124}The folders }{\i\f1 Client}{\f1  and }{\i\f1 Server}{\f1  include the files generated respectively for reusing a component or creating a new component. Each consists of three subdirectories: }{\i\f1 
Include}{\f1  comprises header files needed to compile the Eiffel code, }{\i\f1 CLib}{\f1  contains the generated C and C++ code as well as the library files. }{\i\f1 Component}{\f1  holds Eiffel code that wraps or defines the component. The }{\i\f1 
Component}{\f1  subfolder of }{\i\f1 Server}{\f1  also includes the registration class. This Eiffel class contains the code needed to activate the component; it depends whether the component 
is in-process or out-of-process. You do not need to read or edit the C and C++ code included in }{\i\f1 CLib}{\f1 
 because the wizard automatically compiles it. It is given for information only and can be deleted (you need to keep the library file though). The }{\i\f1 Client}{\f1  and }{\i\f1 Server}{\f1 
 folders also have an Ace file used to compile the generated Eiffel code. In the case of a client, the generated code is precompiled whereas in the case of a server it is compiled in a normal system with the registration class being the root class 
of the system. In the case of an in-process server the Eiffel system is compiled into a DLL whereas in the case of an out-of-process server, it is compiled in a standard executable. 
\par {\*\bkmkstart pgfId_371132}{\*\bkmkend pgfId_371132}The Common folder comprises code that is used for both the server and the client parts. The }{\i\f1 Include}{\f1  and }{\i\f1 CLib}{\f1 
 directories contain respectively the header files and the C and C++ code. The C and C++ sources are not needed and can be deleted, only the library file needs to be kept for the Eiffel system to compile. The }{\i\f1 Interfaces}{\f1 
 subdirectory include Eiffel classes corresponding to the component interfaces and the }{\i\f1 Structures}{\f1  subdirectory includes Eiffel classes wrapping data structures specified in the definition file. 
