{\fs20 \row }\pard \qj\li720\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\adjustright {\f1 {\*\bkmkstart pgfId_373336}{\*\bkmkend pgfId_373336}For both Eiffel coclass and Implemented interfaces, the }{\cs17\i\f1 INTERFACE}{\f1 
 class contains no implementation; it only defines the signatures of the functions that are part of the interface. The actual implementation lies in the heir of that class. 
\par {\*\bkmkstart pgfId_373366}{\*\bkmkend pgfId_373366}How you should use the generated classes in your system depends on whether you want to access an existing component (client) or build a new component in Eiffel (server). 
\par }\pard\plain \s6\qj\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\outlinelevel5\adjustright \b\fs15\cgrid {\f1 {\*\bkmkstart pgfId_371723}{\*\bkmkend pgfId_371723}
\par }\pard\plain \s1\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright \b\f1\fs48\kerning36\cgrid {\page }{\cs26\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s25\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\cs26\super #}{ IDH_accessing_component}}${\footnote \pard\plain 
\s25\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\cs26\super $}{ Accessing a Component}}}{Accessing a Component
\par }\pard\plain \qj\li720\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {\f1 {\*\bkmkstart pgfId_373349}{\*\bkmkend pgfId_373349}
The wizard generates all the necessary code to access the existing component. The plumbing is already done, so instantiating an Eiffel coclass actually initializes COM internals. 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs27\cf9\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart pgfId_371724}{\*\bkmkend pgfId_371724}Using the Generated Code
\par }\pard\plain \qj\li720\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {\f1 {\*\bkmkstart pgfId_373367}{\*\bkmkend pgfId_373367}
To access the component, you need to call features of the coclass. The data types of function arguments are either Eiffel types defined in Eiffel data structure libraries (EiffelBase), stan
dard COM data types defined in the EiffelCOM library, or component COM data types specified in the definition file. For example, from the following IDL line 
\par HRESULT InterfaceFunction ([in] int a, [out, retval] MyStruct * b)
\par {\*\bkmkstart pgfId_373375}{\*\bkmkstart pgfId_373411}{\*\bkmkend pgfId_373375}{\*\bkmkend pgfId_373411}The wizard generates the following feature in the Eiffel coclass: 
\par interface_function (a: }{\i\f1 INTEGER}{\f1 ): }{\i\f1 MY_STRUCT_RECORD}{\f1 
\par {\*\bkmkstart pgfId_373414}{\*\bkmkstart pgfId_373436}{\*\bkmkend pgfId_373414}{\*\bkmkend pgfId_373436}where }{\cs17\i\f1 MY_STRUCT_RECORD}{\f1  is a generated Eiffel class wrapping }{\cs17\i\f1 MyStruct}{\f1 . 
\par Here is a more difficult example:
\par HRESULT a_function ([in] IsomeInterface * p_interface)
\par The wizard generates the following Eiffel feature:
\par a_function (p_interface: }{\i\f1 ISOME_INTERFACE_IMPL_PROXY}{\f1 )
\par where }{\i\f1 ISOME_INTERFACE_IMPL_PROXY}{\f1  is a generated class. Where can you obtain an instance of the class? First, you may receive from other function. Second, you can create it from other }{\i\f1 ECOM_QUERIABLE}{\f1  using the }{\i\f1 
make_from_other}{\f1  feature.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs27\cf9\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart pgfId_373348}{\*\bkmkend pgfId_373348}Contracts
\par }\pard\plain \qj\li720\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {\f1 {\*\bkmkstart pgfId_373448}{\*\bkmkend pgfId_373448}The wizard cannot generate f
ully specified contracts. Indeed, the tool has no domain specific knowledge and can only generate contracts that are domain independent. Such contracts, although useful, are not enough to describe entirely the behavior of the component. Generated contract
s
 include void Eiffel objects as well as C pointer validity (for wrappers) checking. There might be other conditions to allow calls to an Eiffel coclass feature. Invariants and postconditions can be enforced in an heir of the generated Eiffel coclass. Prec
onditions, however, cannot be strengthened. A workaround provided by the wizard is to generate a precondition function for each feature in the interface. The default implementation of these functions always returns }{\cs17\i\f1 True}{\f1 
. They should be redefined to implement the correct behavior: 
\par }\pard\plain \s22\qj\li1440\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\cgrid {\cs18\i interface_function}{ (}{\cs18\i a}{: }{\cs18\i INTEGER}{): }{\cs18\i MY_STRUCT}{ }{\cs19\b is}{ 
