There are a few rules to follow when building an Eiffel coclass client, but they are straightforward and do not add any constraints. First, inherit from the generated Eiffel coclass to implement the preconditions
. Second, any feature call to the Eiffel coclass should include a rescue clause.
\par }\pard \s23\qj\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\adjustright {
\par }\pard\plain \s1\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright \b\f1\fs48\kerning36\cgrid {\page }{\cs26\super #{\footnote \pard\plain \s25\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\cs26\super #}{ IDH_building_component}}${\footnote \pard\plain 
\s25\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\cs26\super $}{ Building a Component}}}{Building a Component
\par }\pard\plain \qj\li720\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {\f1 {\*\bkmkstart pgfId_373966}{\*\bkmkstart pgfId_373983}{\*\bkmkend pgfId_373966}{\*\bkmkend pgfId_373983}The EiffelCOM wizard enables the development of COM co
mponents in Eiffel. To build a COM component you can start either from an IDL file or from an Eiffel project. In the later case the wizard generates an IDL file for you. 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs27\cf9\cgrid {Choosing an Eiffel Class
\par }\pard\plain \s23\qj\li720\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\cgrid {If you start building a COM component from an Eiffel system,
 you need to give an Eiffel class to the wizard. The wizard translates the Flat-Short form of the class into an IDL file, which is a formal specification of the COM component. Clients of the component are able to access only features specified in the IDL 
file. You need to supply the wizard with a Facade class that provides clients with a higher-level, single interface (do not confuse with COM interface) to the facilities of a system.
\par }\pard \s23\li720\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\adjustright {The Facade class should satisfy the following requirements:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s23 \f3\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s23\qj\fi-360\li1800\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1800{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls23\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls23\adjustright {It should h
ave a creation routine \ldblquote make\rdblquote  without arguments. The wizard does not enforce the rule, but breaking it causes a runtime error.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s23 \f3\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s23\fi-360\li1800\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1800{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls23\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls23\adjustright {
Features of the class may only have arguments and return values of the following types: }{\i CHARACTER}{, }{\i INTEGER}{, }{\i REAL}{, }{\i DOUBLE}{, }{\i BOOLEAN}{, }{\i INTEGER_REF}{, }{\i BOOLEAN_REF}{, }{\i REAL_REF}{, }{\i CHARACTER_REF}{, }{\i 
DOUBLE_REF}{, }{\i STRING}{, }{\i DATE_TIME}{, }{\i ECOM_CURRENCY}{, }{\i ECOM_DECIMAL}{, }{\i ECOM_UNKNOWN_INTERFACE}{, }{\i ECOM_AUTOMATION_INTERFACE}{, and }{\i CELL}{ and }{\i ECOM_ARRAY}{ of above types. Features with arguments or return values of o
ther types are excluded from the generated IDL file and are not accessible to the COM runtime.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s23 \f3\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s23\fi-360\li1800\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1800{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls23\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls23\adjustright {
Feature names should not be C++ keywords or names of standard library functions such as min, max, etc. The wizard is able to handle this correctly and to create a COM component; however, other languages may not be able to access it.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s23 \f3\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s23\fi-360\li1800\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1800{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls23\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls23\adjustright {
The class must belong to a compiled Eiffel project.
\par }\pard \s23\li720\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\adjustright {In most Eiffel systems functionality is spread out throughout many classes. A system may not contain a class that can render 
all services of the system and can serve as a Facade to the outside world. If you cannot find such a class you should write one.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs27\cf9\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart pgfId_370371}{\*\bkmkend pgfId_370371}Using the Generated Code
\par }\pard\plain \qj\li360\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {\f1 {\*\bkmkstart pgfId_373984}{\*\bkmkend pgfId_373984}
If you start from an Eiffel project, the wizard produces a ready-to-use component, and you do not need to modify or to implement any generated code.
\par If you start from a COM definition file, you are able to design a more flexible component that has more tha
n one interface and/or coclass and user defined types, but you have to implement features of the generated coclass. The generated Eiffel coclass features are empty. You should redefine them in heir to implement the intended behavior. Unlike client generat
e
d code, the server generated code will differ whether you have chosen to implement an in-process or an out-of-process component. The difference lies in the component activation code in the class ECOM_<Name_of_system>_REGISTRATION. If the component is in-p
rocess then this class includes the four functions that need to be exported from an in-process COM component (}{\i\f1 DllRegisterServer}{\f1 , }{\i\f1 DllUnregisterServer}{\f1 , }{\i\f1 DllGetClassObject}{\f1 , and }{\i\f1 DllCanUnloadNow}{\f1 
). If the component is out-of-process then the registration class includes a feature initializing the component and its graphical user interface. 
\par }\pard \qj\fi360\li360\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\adjustright {\f1 {\*\bkmkstart pgfId_374337}{\*\bkmkend pgfId_374337}
The architecture of generated code for the server is similar to the one for the client: the generated Eiffel coclass should be inherited from and the cont
ract features redefined. The default feature implementation of the generated Eiffel coclass is empty. Features should be redefined to implement the intended behavior. These features will be called by the EiffelCOM runtime whenever a client accesses an int
erface. 
\par }\trowd \trgaph15\trleft660\trbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15 \trbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15 \trbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15 \trbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15 \clvertalc\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15 \cltxlrtb \cellx8700\pard\plain 
\s24\qj\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\intbl\adjustright \f1\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart pgfId_375536}{\*\bkmkend pgfId_375536}
Note: For this first release, the name of the user defined coclass has to be <Name_of_generated_coclass>_IMP. So if the generated coclass name is MY_COCLASS then the user defined coclass name must be MY_COCLASS_IMP.\cell }\pard\plain 
\widctlpar\intbl\adjustright \cgrid {\fs20 \row }\pard\plain \s2\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs27\cf9\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart pgfId_370378}{\*\bkmkstart BM34604}{\*\bkmkend pgfId_370378}{\*\bkmkend BM34604}Component's GUI
\par }\pard\plain \qj\li720\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\adjustright \cgrid {\f1 {\*\bkmkstart pgfId_375550}{\*\bkmkend pgfId_375550}
In the case of an out-of-process server, you might want to add a Graphical User Interface to your component. There are two different scenarios in which the component can be activated: e
ither its user launched it explicitly (e.g. by double clicking the executable icon) or it was launched by the COM runtime to satisfy a client request. The GUI should appear only in the former case, when the user has explicitly launched the application. Th
e generated registration class for an out-of-process server includes the feature: 
\par }\trowd \trgaph15\trleft660\trbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15 \trbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15 \trbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15 \trbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15 \clvertalc\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw15 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw15 \cltxlrtb \cellx5048\pard\plain 
\s22\qj\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\intbl\adjustright \f1\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart pgfId_375560}{\*\bkmkend pgfId_375560}main_window: WEL_FRAME_WINDOW\cell }\pard\plain \widctlpar\intbl\adjustright \cgrid {\fs20 \row }\pard 
\qj\li720\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\adjustright {\f1 {\*\bkmkstart pgfId_375570}{\*\bkmkend pgfId_375570}This feature is a once function that can be redefined in a child class to return the class corr
esponding to the component window. This window is displayed only if COM does not start the component. When COM loads an out-of-process component, it appends the option "-embedding" to the executable. The generated registration class looks for this option 
and if it is part of the process argument list then it sets the default window appearance to hidden. 
\par {\*\bkmkstart pgfId_375583}{\*\bkmkend pgfId_375583}As a summary, when building a server from a COM definition you need to implement classes that inherit from coclasses and implement 
interface functions. The names of the children classes should be the names of the parent classes appended with _}{\i\f1 IMP}{\f1 
. You will also have to inherit from the registration class in the case of an out-of-process component to provide the class that implements the component GUI. 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\sb100\sa100\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs27\cf9\cgrid {{\*\bkmkstart pgfId_375549}{\*\bkmkend pgfId_375549}Exceptions
